chapters/01-cell-file.html:25:<p class="lede">A cobot arm arrives with papers. The gripper, fixture, program and worker beside it create a different safety question. A small integrator must answer that question before commissioning the cell. The attractive business is a site visit, measured evidence and a file that makes the answer defensible. The fatal problem is ownership: Dutch integrators already prepare the risk assessment and CE package, and the manufacturer must carry the declaration. <!-- CHECK: 01-nooteboom --> This is not a cheap document shop. It is a test of whether an integrator will pay an independent specialist to find the dangerous gap, while keeping the customer and the signature.</p>
chapters/01-cell-file.html:52:<p>The answer is a partner and a paper boundary. The Dutch integrator leads operator interviews and supplies the Dutch instructions. The entrant works in English with the engineering lead and in Polish with relevant subcontractors. If the integrator will not own the Dutch-language layer and sign the final declaration, the job fails qualification. The legal sources assign duties to the manufacturer; they do not create a licensed outside CE signatory. <!-- CHECK: 01-credential-gate --> An English-language machinery-safety course is commercially available in the Netherlands, but it is training, not statutory authority. <!-- CHECK: 01-english-training --></p>
