+<p class="lede">A cobot arm arrives with papers. The gripper, fixture, program and worker beside it create a different safety question. A small integrator must answer that question before commissioning the cell. The attractive business is a site visit, measured evidence and a file that makes the answer defensible. At least one Dutch welding-cobot case shows the integrator preparing the risk assessment, technical dossier and CE marking. <!-- CHECK: 01-nooteboom --> The manufacturer remains responsible for the declaration. <!-- CHECK: 01-regulation-duties --> The test is whether an integrator will pay an independent specialist to find the dangerous gap while keeping the customer and signature.</p>
 
 <section class="proposition">
 <h2>The proposition</h2>
@@ -49,18 +49,18 @@
 <p>The engineering habit does the first half of the work. A cell is a system of claims: a hazard maps to a protective measure, the measure maps to a safety function, and the function maps to a test result. The same discipline used to trace a production fault can trace missing evidence. Regulatory literacy helps keep the declaration, risk assessment and technical record separate.</p>
 <p>Polish and Russian matter on the supply side, not as decoration. A Polish subcontractor may have designed a fixture, written a PLC block or supplied an incomplete declaration. The reader can resolve that evidence directly. English can work with export-oriented integrators and their engineering managers. The 30,000-person professional audience can surface partners, but attention is not proof of factory trust.</p>
 <p>The Dutch gate is not passed by claiming that engineers speak English. Instructions must be in a language users understand; Dutch government guidance says a machine manual must use the language of the country where it is marketed. <!-- CHECK: 01-language --> Shop-floor interviews, residual-risk labels and emergency behaviour can also turn on ordinary Dutch. A2 is not enough to own those conversations.</p>
-<p>The answer is a partner and a paper boundary. The Dutch integrator leads operator interviews and supplies the Dutch instructions. The entrant works in English with the engineering lead and in Polish with relevant subcontractors. If the integrator will not own the Dutch-language layer and sign the final declaration, the job fails qualification. The legal sources assign duties to the manufacturer; they do not create a licensed outside CE signatory. <!-- CHECK: 01-credential-gate --> An English-language machinery-safety course is commercially available in the Netherlands, but it is training, not statutory authority. <!-- CHECK: 01-english-training --></p>
-<p>The precedent is Cobots and Machinery Safety Ltd in Corby. Its site sells cobot risk assessments, force-and-pressure testing and CE/UKCA support. <!-- CHECK: 01-precedent-offer --> Companies House independently records an active engineering consultancy, incorporated on 10 July 2019, with accounts through July 2025 and a June 2026 confirmation statement. <!-- CHECK: 01-precedent-trace --> That proves a small specialist can persist on the shape. It does not prove Dutch prices or demand.</p>
+<p>The answer is a partner and a paper boundary. The Dutch integrator leads operator interviews and supplies the Dutch instructions. The entrant works in English with the engineering lead and in Polish with relevant subcontractors. If the integrator will not own the Dutch-language layer and sign the final declaration, the job fails qualification. The legal sources assign duties to the manufacturer; they do not create a licensed outside CE signatory. <!-- CHECK: 01-credential-gate --> In 2017, a Dutch provider advertised that its machinery-safety and CE-marking course could be delivered in English. <!-- CHECK: 01-english-training --> That historical offer is evidence of possibility, not current availability or statutory authority. Current dates, duration and price need direct confirmation before the pilot.</p>
+<p>The precedent is Cobots and Machinery Safety Ltd in Corby. Its site markets calibrated force-and-pressure testing and documentation supporting CE/UKCA marking. <!-- CHECK: 01-precedent-offer --> Companies House independently records an active engineering consultancy, incorporated on 10 July 2019, with accounts through July 2025 and a June 2026 confirmation statement. <!-- CHECK: 01-precedent-trace --> Together, these records confirm that an active small engineering consultancy currently markets part of the shape. They do not establish revenue, tenure of the offer, headcount, commercial viability, Dutch prices or Dutch demand.</p>
 <p>The constraint that breaks the fit is travel. Measurements happen at a commissioned cell, often during a production stop. If the reader cannot reserve site days alongside the job, regulatory reading has no economic value here. The other break is appetite for liability: a findings report is still professional work around moving machinery.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="sale">
 <h2>The first sale</h2>
 <p>Build ten named prospect types, all narrow enough to have repeat cells: cobot dealers, welding-cell integrators, palletising specialists, machine-tending integrators, packaging-line builders, food-handling automation shops, vision-and-guarding integrators, mobile-robot integrators, retrofit control shops and Polish integrators selling into the Netherlands. Exclude a general factory that has built one experimental cell. It creates no repeat channel.</p>
-<p>The opening offer is a two-stage paid diagnostic for one cell already installed but not finally accepted. Stage one is a desk review at a <strong>€1,250 design hurdle</strong>, credited against stage two. It checks scope, declarations, drawings, risk logic and planned tests. Stage two is the site challenge and evidence pack at a <strong>€4,500 total design hurdle</strong>. The integrator pays 50% before the desk review and the balance before the site date.</p>
+<p>The opening offer is a two-stage paid diagnostic for one cell already installed but not finally accepted. Stage one is a desk review at a <strong>€1,250 design hurdle</strong>, credited against stage two. It checks scope, declarations, drawings, risk logic and planned tests. Stage two is the site challenge and evidence pack at a <strong>€4,500 pilot design hurdle</strong>. The invoice hurdles are €625 at booking, €625 when the desk review is delivered and €3,250 before the pilot site visit. At the <strong>€5,500 mature design hurdle</strong>, the last invoice becomes €4,250. Prices exclude VAT.</p>
 <p>The owner or engineering lead signs because a delayed acceptance, missing test or disputed responsibility can consume senior engineering time. The integrator retains the end-customer contract. It introduces the entrant as independent validation support, not the CE authority. The entrant will not begin without the named manufacturer, cell boundary, intended use, Dutch interview lead and authority to see the relevant design evidence.</p>
 <p>Demand outreach should be concrete. Send ten English or Polish notes showing one anonymised evidence map and ask who reviewed the last awkward cell. Ask what the integrator did internally, what an outsider did, what delayed acceptance and what the outsider cost. Do not lead with the 2027 Regulation. The sale is about an installed cell and an acceptance date.</p>
-<p>The online record does not settle the pitch's kill fact. IAB publishes €6,000 for CE work on a simple machine and about €30,000 for a machinery assembly, both including a manual. <!-- CHECK: 01-incumbent-price --> Those are broad anchors, not cobot-cell quotes. The named Dutch integrator and safety-bureau pages reviewed here publish scope but no cell-file price. <!-- CHECK: 01-price-boundary --> The record therefore cannot exclude a dealer bundling the work or a competent bureau quoting below €3,000.</p>
+<p>The online record does not settle the pitch's kill fact. IAB gives undated, potentially stale examples of €6,000 for CE work on a simple machine and about €30,000 for a machinery assembly. <!-- CHECK: 01-incumbent-price --> Both include a manual, and neither is a cobot-cell quote. The reviewed Pilz page lists machinery-safety services but no public prices in its captured text. <!-- CHECK: 01-price-boundary --> Absence on one page does not exclude a private quote. The record therefore cannot exclude a dealer bundling the work or a competent bureau quoting below the <strong>€3,000 kill hurdle</strong>.</p>
 </section>
 
